<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 1550–1070 BCE (the New Kingdom)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Pharaoh to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="Abu Simbel"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a><span class="soc-credit">youssef_alam · CC BY 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Egyptian society was ranked by office and by closeness to the king. At its head stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, in theory the owner of all the land and the only link between the gods and the people. Egyptian texts set the elite, the pat, above the rekhyt, the common people. The elite were the literate officials who ran the state and the priests who ran the temples and their estates; writing was the key to both, and sons usually followed their fathers into office. Below them came scribes, soldiers and craftsmen, and then the farmers, the great majority, who owed the state taxes and labour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>’s wars brought in captives who were made slaves. The law treated free men and women of every rank much alike, and any peasant might petition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, but the ladder was steep: a small literate circle governed some two to three million people.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 1550–1070 BCE (the New Kingdom)</span><span><b>People</b> 2–3 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 8</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The pharaoh</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">High officials</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Priests</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Scribes</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Soldiers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Artisans</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:100%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Farmers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
